{
  "description": "The four SPAKE2 test vectors of RFC 9382 Appendix B, over the P256-SHA256-HKDF-HMAC ciphersuite. RFC 9382 publishes vectors only for P-256, so these gate the group-generic SPAKE2 core in spake2-core.ts before its edwards25519 instantiation (docs/bridge-pairing-protocol.md \u00a73, \u00a713) is trusted. All byte strings are lowercase hex; A and B are the RFC identity strings, and points use the SEC1 uncompressed encoding with its 0x04 prefix.",
  "source": "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9382.txt, Appendix B, parsed programmatically (the RFC line-wraps long hex values).",
  "ttProvenance": "TT is transcribed from the RFC rather than generated. Deriving it by running our own implementation would make the transcript assertion circular and defeat the gate: the point of these vectors is to prove the TT layout, key schedule, and confirmation MACs independently of our code. Three cross-checks back the transcription: (1) each vector's Ke || Ka equals the Hash(TT) the RFC prints separately; (2) each TT was reconstructed in Python as enc(A) || enc(B) || enc(pA) || enc(pB) || enc(K) || enc(I2OSP(w,32)) from that same vector's own pA/pB/K/w, independent of this repository's encoders; (3) every field except TT is byte-identical to the independent copy in the reference vector generator that produced docs/bridge-pairing-protocol-vectors.json.",
